\f1\fs22 In the lobby, Sarah took Chanta by the hand and led him insistently down a narrow hall by the cloakroom.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Where are we going for dinner?" Chanta asked, hope restoring his speech.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We\'92re not,\'94 Sarah said brusquely.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta\'92s crankcase seized. The sump fell out of his belly. \'93Then\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah hesitated.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Even Chanta could see there was something she needed to get off her chest. Chanta stared at her chest. \'93Just show me\'97I mean, just tell me. What is it?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah swallowed. \'93When you were cleaning that office this morning\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta tightened his eyebrows with confusion.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The suicide,\'94 Sarah prompted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What about it?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Did you take any documents from the office? Maybe something spattered that you assumed should be binned?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Nah, I never took any documents.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You never took anything?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Nah.\'94 But guilt betrayed his face. Never mind it stemmed from stealing from a dead man on the pavement\'97he was a thief, even if his spoils amounted to a disc seemingly punched from an old shoe sole. Hardly worth confessing to a woman he\'92d met that morning. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tony, I need the things you took, okay? They\'92re important.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I didn\'92t take anything!\'94 he protested.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Maybe TRAMPS has them, as evidence. Tony. If\'85 anyone\'85 harasses you about stealing documents from our office\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just tell them the police have them.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta snorted. \'93You gotta be kidding.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Why?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Alex\'97\'94 And there Chanta stopped himself and chewed on his next words. \'93I know how TRAMPS work with these things: they take one look, declare it a suicide, call me, then walk straight out.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah nodded to the probable truth of that.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sarah? What\'92s this about?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah swallowed. \'93Not long after you left, a PAPER agent rifled through the office, desperate for documents. He even accused me of taking them, for tax sake.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The mention of PAPER chilled Chanta\'92s blood. \'93What\'92s this got to do with me?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He noticed the freshly cleaned carpet and figured a cleaner had taken what he wanted.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So you put PAPER on my arse?\'94 Chanta enjoyed being indelicate, especially about the most feared secret service among many in the country.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I didn\'92t! He figured it out. Look, I came to warn you\'97I spent most of the afternoon tracking you down. Do you know there\'92s almost no data about you anywhere? And what do I find? Another agent calmly sipping beer with you!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Who?\'94 Even Chanta could discount Abe and Major. \'93Halo?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah nodded. \'93He\'92s got cyborg implants.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta cackled. This beautiful angel was a conspiracy nut. \'93And you know this how? Got a cyborg sniffer in your handbag? Do cyborgs smell more or less like chicken?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93His eyes. You look closely at his eyes, you can see he uses  empathy tech.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Empty tea...? What\'92s tea got to do with anything?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Em-pa-thy tech. I bet Halo\'92s an extraordinarily good pick-up up artist.\'94 Sarah saw the truth land with Chanta.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, we always wondered how he kept pulling it off. Never went home alone. But empty\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Of course you\'92ve never heard of it. You\'92re Cloudborne\'97this is Airborne gear. The toy versions change your pupil size, your iris colour\'97party tricks. The real gear reads every twitch in someone\'92s face and plays them until they can\'92t say no.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta blinked. \'93You said recreational?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah pursed her lips. She shouldn\'92t tell a Cloudborne this, but it probably wouldn\'92t matter. \'93Ever wonder about Airborne do-gooders who set up schools for disadvantaged kids?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta took a second. \'93Every Airborne teacher uses empty tea tech.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93There\'92s a whole consumer industry for Airborne exploiting Cloudborne however they want.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta replayed her words. \'93They target our kids,\'94 he said, setting his jaw for vengeance.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah nodded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93But Halo\'92s no\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93His tastes may differ, but the game\'92s the same.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How does this prove he\'92s a PAPER agent?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I knew the second I looked him in the eye. It took all my awareness to break rapport. His tech is much higher grade than you need for Cloudborne juveniles. Only certain agencies have access to gear that sophisticated.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93If it\'92s so restricted, how\'92s a dreg like Halo come by it?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m telling you he\'92s a PAPER agent. And he\'92s Airborne.\'94
